1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:01,800
(Sheldon:)Of course.

2
00:00:01,963 --> 00:00:06,963
(Sheldon:)First, consider an ordinary magnet,
which has, as even the most uneducated...

3
00:00:07,135 --> 00:00:10,015
[VOICE BECOMING HIGH-PITCHED]
...in your audience must know, two poles...

4
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:11,560
[CLEARS THROAT]

5
00:00:11,973 --> 00:00:15,103
(Sheldon:)...a north and south pole.

6
00:00:15,268 --> 00:00:18,308
(Sheldon:)If you cut that in half,
you have two smaller magnets...

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:21,320
(Sheldon:)...each with its own north
and south pole.

8
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,820
(Ira:)Dr. Cooper, I think there might be
something wrong with our connection.

9
00:00:25,987 --> 00:00:29,077
(Sheldon(even higher pitch):)No, I hear you fine.

10
00:00:29,241 --> 00:00:33,121
(Sheldon(even higher pitch):)As I was saying,
an ordinary magnet has two poles.

11
00:00:33,286 --> 00:00:35,626
(Sheldon(even higher pitch):)The primary characteristic
of a monopole...

12
00:00:35,789 --> 00:00:40,249
(Sheldon(even higher pitch):)...is that it has only one pole,
hence, "monopole."

13
00:00:41,211 --> 00:00:45,091
SHELDON: A requirement for string theory,
or M-theory, if you will...

14
00:00:45,257 --> 00:00:47,797
(Sheldon:)...is the existence of such monopoles.

15
00:00:48,343 --> 00:00:52,093
(Sheldon:)I myself led an expedition to the
Arctic Circle in search of said particles.

16
00:00:52,264 --> 00:00:54,594
(Sheldon:)Kripke, I found the nozzle.
I'm going to kill you.

17
00:00:54,766 --> 00:01:02,026
[ALL LAUGHING]

